const PATTERN_ACADEMY_9 = {
  "combinations": {
    title: "Pattern 47 — Combinations: startIndex, Capacity Pruning & the Reuse Rule (Must Master)",
    sections: [
      { type: "callout", heading: "The core idea", tone: "gold",
        body: "Deceptively simple, but extremely important because startIndex becomes one of your core backtracking tools.",
        conclusion: "The central idea: choose elements in increasing index order so different orderings of the same selection are never generated." },
      { type: "text", heading: "1. What is a combination?",
        body: "Suppose nums = [1,2,3,4], k = 2. We want every group of 2: [1,2],[1,3],[1,4],[2,3],[2,4],[3,4]. We do NOT want [2,1],[3,1],[4,1] because combinations don't care about order. So: [1,2] == [2,1]." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "2. The core mental model", dark: true,
        body: "At every recursion level: choose one element from the remaining suffix.\n\nExample [1,2,3,4]. Start: 1 2 3 4 -- choose. If you choose 2, the next choice can only come from 3 4. Never go backward to 1.",
        conclusion: "Therefore: startIndex is the mechanism that prevents permutations." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "3. The most important rule", tone: "gold",
        body: "For combinations: choose i -> recurse(i + 1). That i+1 means: never choose an earlier element again.",
        conclusion: "This guarantees 1 -> 2 can happen, but 2 -> 1 cannot. Therefore we generate [1,2] only once." },
      { type: "chips", heading: "4. Recognition triggers",
        intro: "When you see:",
        items: ["choose K elements", "choose K numbers", "all groups of size K", "select K items", "combinations", "order doesn't matter", "choose from a range", "choose a subset of fixed size"],
        note: "Think: Backtracking + startIndex." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "5. Universal combination template", dark: true,
        body: "void backtrack(int start) {\n\n    if (path.size() == k) {\n        result.add(new ArrayList<>(path));\n        return;\n    }\n\n    for (int i = start; i < nums.length; i++) {\n\n        path.add(nums[i]);\n\n        backtrack(i + 1);\n\n        path.remove(path.size() - 1);\n    }\n}\n\nOne of the most important templates in your interview preparation." },
      { type: "text", heading: "6. Walk through [1,2,3,4], k=2",
        body: "Start: path=[], start=0. Choose 1: path=[1], start=1, available: 2 3 4. Choose 2: [1,2]. Record. Undo: [1]. Choose 3: [1,3]. Choose 4: [1,4]. Return.\n\nChoose 2 at root: [2]. Now only 3 4. So [2,3],[2,4]. Then [3,4]. Done." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "7. Visualize the tree", dark: true,
        body: "                         []\n                /        |        |       \\\n               1         2        3        4\n             / | \\      / \\       |\n            2  3  4    3   4      4\n\nNotice: there is no 2->1, 3->1, 4->1 because startIndex only moves forward. That is the entire mechanism." },
      { type: "comparison", heading: "8. Why not visited[]?", items: [
        { label: "Permutations", body: "[1,2] and [2,1] are different. So: visited[] is required." },
        { label: "Combinations", body: "[1,2] and [2,1] are identical. So we prevent going backward using: startIndex." }
      ]},
      { type: "diagram", heading: "9. Golden rule", dark: true,
        body: "Order matters\n    |\nPermutation\n    |\nvisited[]\n\nOrder doesn't matter\n    |\nCombination\n    |\nstartIndex\n\nYou should be able to answer this instantly in an interview." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "10. Combinations from 1...n", dark: true,
        body: "Classic problem: given n and k, return all combinations of k numbers from [1,n]. Example n=4, k=2 -> [1,2],[1,3],[1,4],[2,3],[2,4],[3,4].\n\nvoid backtrack(int start) {\n\n    if (path.size() == k) {\n        result.add(new ArrayList<>(path));\n        return;\n    }\n\n    for (int i = start; i <= n; i++) {\n\n        path.add(i);\n\n        backtrack(i + 1);\n\n        path.remove(path.size() - 1);\n    }\n}" },
      { type: "callout", heading: "11-12. Why start instead of index? & startIndex as a constraint", tone: "gold",
        body: "Because start means: the earliest candidate I'm allowed to choose next. Think: start = boundary of allowed choices.\n\nSuppose path=[2], then startIndex=3 means allowed: 3,4,5,... not allowed: 1,2.",
        conclusion: "So startIndex is actually a constraint on the search space. This is why it prevents duplicate orderings." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "13-14. Combination size pruning & remaining capacity", tone: "gold",
        body: "Suppose n=100, k=10, start=95, path.size()=9 -- need one more element, fine. But suppose path.size()=5, start=98 -- need 5 more elements, but only 98,99,100 remain. Impossible. So we can prune.\n\nAt any point: remainingNeeded = k - path.size(). Available elements: n - i + 1. If available < remainingNeeded, don't continue.",
        conclusion: "This is: Capacity pruning." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "15-16. Optimized loop", dark: true,
        body: "Instead of: for (int i = start; i <= n; i++)\n\nyou can use: for (int i = start; i <= n - (k - path.size()) + 1; i++)\n\nWhy? Because after choosing i, there must still be enough elements left to complete the combination. A nice optimization to discuss if the interviewer asks.\n\nExample: n=5, k=3, path=[1], need 2 more. If you choose i=5, there are no elements after 5 -- impossible, so don't even try 5. The loop can stop earlier." },
      { type: "text", heading: "17. Pattern: Combinations With Conditions",
        body: "Suppose: choose k numbers whose sum equals target. Now state becomes: start, path, remainingTarget. At each choice: remainingTarget -= nums[i], then backtrack(i+1), and undo: remainingTarget += nums[i]. This leads directly to: Combination Sum." },
      { type: "comparison", heading: "18. Combination vs Combination Sum", items: [
        { label: "Combination", body: "Usually fixed size: choose k elements. Termination: path.size() == k." },
        { label: "Combination Sum", body: "Usually target-driven: sum == target. Termination: remainingTarget == 0. And often: remainingTarget < 0 -> prune." }
      ]},
      { type: "callout", heading: "19. Combination Sum reuse rule", tone: "red",
        body: "Suppose candidates=[2,3,6,7], target=7. Can reuse 2: 2+2+3=7.",
        conclusion: "So: backtrack(i), not backtrack(i+1). This is extremely important." },
      { type: "variants", heading: "20. The three backtrack calls", items: [
        { id: "1", title: "Combination, no reuse", note: "backtrack(i + 1)" },
        { id: "2", title: "Combination Sum, reuse allowed", note: "backtrack(i)" },
        { id: "3", title: "Permutation", note: "backtrack() + visited[]" }
      ], conclusion: "This one table is extremely valuable." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "21. Combination with duplicates", dark: true,
        body: "Suppose nums=[1,2,2,3], k=2. You want: [1,2],[1,3],[2,2],[2,3] -- not [1,2] twice.\n\nSort first: Arrays.sort(nums); then:\nif (i > start && nums[i] == nums[i - 1]) {\n    continue;\n}\n\nAgain: skip duplicate choices at the same recursion level." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "22. Why this doesn't remove [2,2]", tone: "gold",
        body: "Suppose nums=[1,2a,2b,3]. At root, 2a and 2b are duplicate starting choices -- skip 2b. But after choosing 2a (path=[2a]), we can choose 2b to form [2,2].",
        conclusion: "So the duplicate check only applies i > start at the current level." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "23. Pattern: Combination + Sorting + Pruning", dark: true,
        body: "A very common compound pattern:\nSort -> Backtracking -> Skip duplicates -> Prune\n\nYou'll see it in: Combination Sum II, subsets with duplicates, some partitioning problems." },
      { type: "comparison", heading: "24. Combination vs Subset", items: [
        { label: "Subset", body: "Any size: 0 -> N. Example: [], [1], [1,2], ... doesn't require path size K." },
        { label: "Combination", body: "Fixed size: K. Example: choose exactly 3." }
      ]},
      { type: "text", heading: "25. Combination vs Subsequence",
        body: "Subsequence: preserve order. Combination: order doesn't matter. Example: [1,3] is a subsequence of [1,2,3]. But combination [1,3] is a selection independent of the original sequence semantics. The implementation may look similar, but the problem semantics are different." },
      { type: "text", heading: "26. Pattern: Combination With Exact Constraints",
        body: "Suppose: choose exactly 4 numbers with sum 20 and maximum value <= 8. State: path, startIndex, sum. At each choice: if value > 8, skip. Then: sum += value, backtrack(i+1), sum -= value. This demonstrates the general formula: Choice + Constraint + Recursion + Undo." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "27. Pattern: Combination With Minimum/Maximum", tone: "gold",
        body: "Suppose: find all combinations of K elements whose sum is at least target. Now pruning may be based on bounds. If even choosing the largest possible remaining values cannot reach target: PRUNE. More advanced.",
        conclusion: "The principle: use what you know about the remaining search space to prove impossibility." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "28. Pattern: Combination + Memoization", tone: "gold",
        body: "Suppose the question isn't \"return all combinations\" but \"how many combinations produce target?\" Now repeated states may occur. State might be (index, remainingTarget). Then: memo[index][remainingTarget] can avoid recomputation.",
        conclusion: "This transitions from Backtracking to DP. This connection is extremely important for your DP preparation." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "29. The bigger picture", dark: true,
        body: "                   BACKTRACKING\n                         |\n          +--------------+--------------+\n          v              v              v\n       Subsets      Combinations    Permutations\n          |              |              |\n       take/skip       startIndex      visited\n                         |\n                         v\n                  Combination Sum\n                         |\n                  +------+------+\n                  v             v\n               reuse         no reuse\n                  v             v\n                i             i + 1" },
      { type: "variants", heading: "30. The most important recognition table", items: [
        { id: "1", title: "All selections", note: "Subsets -- core state: index" },
        { id: "2", title: "Choose exactly K", note: "Combinations -- core state: startIndex" },
        { id: "3", title: "Order matters", note: "Permutations -- core state: visited[]" },
        { id: "4", title: "Target sum", note: "Combination Sum -- core state: remaining" },
        { id: "5", title: "Reuse allowed", note: "Combination Sum -- recurse i" },
        { id: "6", title: "No reuse", note: "Combination -- recurse i+1" },
        { id: "7", title: "Duplicate values", note: "Sort + skip -- i > start" },
        { id: "8", title: "Impossible branch", note: "Prune -- constraints" },
        { id: "9", title: "Count instead of enumerate", note: "DP candidate -- state memoization" }
      ]},
      { type: "callout", heading: "31. The interview decision process", tone: "gold",
        body: "Suppose interviewer gives you: \"given an array, select numbers...\" Don't immediately code. Ask:\n\nQ1: Does order matter? YES -> Permutation. NO -> Combination/Subsets.\nQ2: Must size be exactly K? YES -> Combination. NO -> Subsets.\nQ3: Can an element be reused? YES -> recurse(i). NO -> recurse(i+1).\nQ4: Are there duplicates? YES -> sort + skip same-level duplicates.\nQ5: Is there a target/constraint? YES -> add pruning.",
        conclusion: "That decision tree is far more valuable than memorizing 20 LeetCode solutions." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "32. Muscle-memory card", dark: true,
        body: "When you hear: \"Choose K elements, order doesn't matter.\" Immediately think: backtrack(start).\n\nThen:\nfor (int i = start; i < n; i++) {\n\n    path.add(nums[i]);\n\n    backtrack(i + 1);\n\n    path.remove(path.size() - 1);\n}\n\nIf duplicates: if (i > start && nums[i] == nums[i - 1]) continue;\nIf reuse is allowed: backtrack(i)\nIf order matters: visited[]" },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "33. One thing to practice", dark: true,
        body: "Before solving any combination problem, draw the first 2 levels of the recursion tree.\n\nFor [1,2,3,4], k=2:\n[]\n+-- 1\n|   +-- 2\n|   +-- 3\n|   +-- 4\n+-- 2\n|   +-- 3\n|   +-- 4\n+-- 3\n|   +-- 4\n+-- 4\n\nNotice: 1 -> 2 works, 2 -> 1 does not, because startIndex moves forward. If you can visualize this, the code becomes almost automatic." },
      { type: "checklist", heading: "Tier 5 progress",
        items: [{ label: "37. Basic Trie", done: true }, { label: "38. Trie + Prefix Search", done: true },
          { label: "39. Trie + DFS", done: true }, { label: "40. Trie + Backtracking", done: true },
          { label: "41. Trie + Wildcard", done: true }, { label: "42. Trie + Frequency", done: true },
          { label: "43. Bitwise Trie", done: false }, { label: "44. Basic Backtracking", done: true },
          { label: "45. Subsets", done: true }, { label: "46. Permutations", done: true },
          { label: "47. Combinations", done: true }, { label: "48. Combination Sum", done: false },
          { label: "49. Partitioning", done: false }, { label: "50. Grid Backtracking", done: false },
          { label: "51. Constraint Backtracking", done: false }, { label: "52. Backtracking + Trie", done: false },
          { label: "53. Backtracking + Bitmask", done: false }] },
      { type: "nextLesson", pattern: "Combination Sum",
        points: ["Where the backtracking framework becomes much more powerful: startIndex + remaining target + reuse/no reuse + sorting + duplicate handling + pruning", "Combination Sum, Combination Sum II, Combination Sum III and similar-looking problems are actually variations of one underlying template"] }
    ]
  },

  "combination-sum": {
    title: "Pattern 48 — Combination Sum: Reuse Rules, Bound Pruning & the DP Trap (Must Master)",
    sections: [
      { type: "callout", heading: "The core idea", tone: "gold",
        body: "One of the most important backtracking families for coding interviews because it teaches you how to combine: Backtracking + startIndex + target/remaining + reuse rules + pruning + duplicate handling.",
        conclusion: "You'll learn how to recognize different Combination Sum problems as variations of one template." },
      { type: "text", heading: "1. Why does this pattern exist?",
        body: "Suppose candidates = [2,3,6,7], target = 7. Find all combinations whose sum is 7. Answer: [2,2,3], [7]. The challenge is: which number should I choose next? That is a backtracking decision." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "2. Mental model", dark: true,
        body: "Maintain: path, remainingTarget, startIndex.\n\nExample target=7. Choose 2: remaining=5. Choose 2: remaining=3. Choose 3: remaining=0. Solution: [2,2,3]. Then backtrack." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "3-4. Core template & reuse vs no reuse", dark: true,
        body: "For Combination Sum I, where values can be reused:\n\nvoid backtrack(int start, int remaining) {\n\n    if (remaining == 0) {\n        result.add(new ArrayList<>(path));\n        return;\n    }\n\n    if (remaining < 0) {\n        return;\n    }\n\n    for (int i = start; i < candidates.length; i++) {\n\n        path.add(candidates[i]);\n\n        backtrack(i, remaining - candidates[i]);\n\n        path.remove(path.size() - 1);\n    }\n}\n\nThe most important line: backtrack(i, ...) -- because reuse is allowed." },
      { type: "comparison", heading: "4b. Reuse allowed vs not allowed", items: [
        { label: "Reuse allowed", body: "backtrack(i, ...). Example: 2 -> 2 -> 2 -> 2 is allowed." },
        { label: "Reuse not allowed", body: "backtrack(i + 1, ...). Example: after 2, next choices start after 2." }
      ], conclusion: "This single difference changes the problem. One of the most important rules in the entire backtracking family." },
      { type: "text", heading: "5. Combination Sum I",
        body: "Problem: candidates=[2,3,6,7], target=7. Each number can be used unlimited times. Therefore: backtrack(i, remaining). Answers: [2,2,3], [7]." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "6-7. Why don't we generate [3,2,2]? & canonical ordering", tone: "gold",
        body: "Because after choosing 3, we don't go backward. Suppose [2,3] is generated -- we don't later generate [3,2] because startIndex only moves forward. Thus [2,3,2] isn't generated either. Instead [2,2,3] is the canonical ordering.\n\nWe enforce: indices selected i1 <= i2 <= i3 <= ... Therefore [2,2,3] is generated once, but [2,3,2] and [3,2,2] are not.",
        conclusion: "This prevents duplicate permutations. This is how startIndex controls the search space." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "8. The recursion tree", dark: true,
        body: "For [2,3,6,7], target=7:\n\n                     []\n             /       |       \\\n            2        3        6...\n          / | \\      |\n         2  3  6     3...\n        / \\\n       2   3\n           |\n           0\n\nWhenever remaining == 0, we found a solution. Whenever remaining < 0, we prune." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "9-10. Pruning & optimized template", tone: "gold",
        body: "Suppose remaining=3 and candidate 6. Since 6 > 3, you can't use it. If candidates are sorted [2,3,6,7], then once you hit 6 > remaining, you can break because everything after 6 is even larger.",
        conclusion: "This is why sorting is useful." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "10b. Optimized template", dark: true,
        body: "Arrays.sort(candidates);\n\nvoid backtrack(int start, int remaining) {\n\n    if (remaining == 0) {\n        result.add(new ArrayList<>(path));\n        return;\n    }\n\n    for (int i = start; i < candidates.length; i++) {\n\n        if (candidates[i] > remaining) {\n            break;\n        }\n\n        path.add(candidates[i]);\n\n        backtrack(i, remaining - candidates[i]);\n\n        path.remove(path.size() - 1);\n    }\n}\n\nNotice we don't even need remaining < 0 because candidate > remaining is already pruned." },
      { type: "text", heading: "11. Combination Sum II",
        body: "Now candidates=[10,1,2,7,6,1,5], target=8. Each element can be used at most once. Answer: [1,1,6], [1,2,5], [1,7], [2,6]. Now we have NO REUSE. Therefore: backtrack(i + 1, ...)." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "12. Why sorting is mandatory here", tone: "red",
        body: "Sort: [1,1,2,5,6,7,10]. Now duplicates are adjacent. At the same recursion level, 1 and 1 would create duplicate branches.",
        conclusion: "So: if (i > start && candidates[i] == candidates[i - 1]) continue; -- don't use the same value as another sibling choice." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "13. Combination Sum II template", dark: true,
        body: "Arrays.sort(candidates);\n\nvoid backtrack(int start, int remaining) {\n\n    if (remaining == 0) {\n        result.add(new ArrayList<>(path));\n        return;\n    }\n\n    for (int i = start; i < candidates.length; i++) {\n\n        if (candidates[i] > remaining) {\n            break;\n        }\n\n        if (i > start &&\n            candidates[i] == candidates[i - 1]) {\n            continue;\n        }\n\n        path.add(candidates[i]);\n\n        backtrack(i + 1, remaining - candidates[i]);\n\n        path.remove(path.size() - 1);\n    }\n}\n\nA must-know template." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "14. Understand the duplicate rule", tone: "gold",
        body: "Suppose [1a,1b,2]. At the root: 1a, 1b, 2. We only want one branch starting with 1. So 1a -> use, 1b -> skip. But after [1a], we can still choose 1b to form [1,1].",
        conclusion: "Therefore i > start is crucial." },
      { type: "text", heading: "15-16. Combination Sum III",
        body: "Problem: find combinations of k numbers from 1..9 that sum to n. Example: k=3, n=7. Answer: [1,2,4]. Rules: numbers 1 through 9, no reuse, exactly K numbers, target sum. State: start, path.size(), remaining." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "16b. Combination Sum III template", dark: true,
        body: "void backtrack(\n    int start,\n    int remaining\n) {\n\n    if (path.size() == k) {\n\n        if (remaining == 0) {\n            result.add(new ArrayList<>(path));\n        }\n\n        return;\n    }\n\n    for (int i = start; i <= 9; i++) {\n\n        if (i > remaining) {\n            break;\n        }\n\n        path.add(i);\n\n        backtrack(i + 1, remaining - i);\n\n        path.remove(path.size() - 1);\n    }\n}\n\nNotice: i + 1 because reuse isn't allowed." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "17. Combination Sum IV — completely different", tone: "red",
        body: "Important interview trap. Problem: given numbers and target, count the number of combinations that sum to target, where ORDER MATTERS. Example: nums=[1,2], target=3. Answers: [1,2], [2,1]. So count: 2.",
        conclusion: "This is NOT the same backtracking pattern -- because [1,2] != [2,1], order matters. This strongly suggests: DP." },
      { type: "comparison", heading: "18. Combination Sum vs Combination Sum IV", items: [
        { label: "Combination Sum", body: "Order doesn't matter: [2,3] = [3,2]. Use: Backtracking + startIndex." },
        { label: "Combination Sum IV", body: "Order matters: [2,3] != [3,2]. Use: 1D DP." }
      ], conclusion: "This is an extremely important distinction." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "19. Why DP for Combination Sum IV?", tone: "gold",
        body: "Define dp[t] as: number of ordered combinations that sum to t. Then: dp[t] += dp[t - num]. For nums=[1,2], target=3: dp[0]=1, dp[1]=dp[0], dp[2]=dp[1]+dp[0], dp[3]=dp[2]+dp[1]. Result: 2.",
        conclusion: "This is one of your first examples where changing whether order matters changes Backtracking into DP." },
      { type: "variants", heading: "20. The big Combination Sum family", items: [
        { id: "1", title: "Combination Sum I", note: "Reuse: Yes. Order: No. Main pattern: Backtracking" },
        { id: "2", title: "Combination Sum II", note: "Reuse: No. Order: No. Main pattern: Backtracking + duplicates" },
        { id: "3", title: "Combination Sum III", note: "Reuse: No. Order: No. Main pattern: Backtracking + fixed K" },
        { id: "4", title: "Combination Sum IV", note: "Reuse: Yes. Order: YES. Main pattern: DP" }
      ], conclusion: "This is extremely high-value interview knowledge." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "21-22. Exact target + positive numbers / negative numbers", tone: "red",
        body: "If all candidates > 0, then remaining < 0 means impossible -- return. This is safe because adding more positive numbers can never decrease the sum.\n\nBut suppose [-1, 2, 3]. Now remaining < 0 does NOT necessarily mean impossible -- you could add -1 and become positive again.",
        conclusion: "Never apply a pruning rule without proving its assumption. A very important senior-interview habit." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "23. Reuse + negative numbers", tone: "red",
        body: "If unlimited reuse is allowed and negative numbers exist: [-1,1], target=0 -- you can have infinitely many combinations: [-1,1], [-1,-1,1,1], ... So constraints matter enormously.",
        conclusion: "Always check: are values positive? Can values repeat? Is target bounded? Is there a maximum length? Don't blindly apply the standard template." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "24-25. Bound pruning", tone: "gold",
        body: "Suppose Combination Sum III: k=3, remaining=20, and only 8,9 remain -- you need three numbers but only two candidates. Prune.\n\nMore advanced: compute minimum possible sum and maximum possible sum from remaining candidates. If target is outside that range: PRUNE.\n\nExample: need 3 numbers, remaining candidates 5,6,7,8. Minimum: 5+6+7=18. Maximum: 8+7+6=21. If remainingTarget=30 -> impossible (30>21). If remainingTarget=10 -> impossible (10<18).",
        conclusion: "This is called: Bound pruning. An advanced optimization." },
      { type: "text", heading: "26. Combination Sum + Memoization",
        body: "If the question is \"how many ways can I reach target?\" rather than \"return every combination,\" then consider remainingTarget as the state. If choices don't depend on the exact path, dp[remaining] may be enough. This is where backtracking naturally evolves into DP." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "27. Critical interview question", tone: "gold",
        body: "When you see \"find all combinations...\" think: Backtracking. When you see \"count the number of combinations...\" ask: does order matter? Are states repeated? Then DP may be better. When you see \"return the minimum/maximum...\" ask: can I formulate a reusable state?",
        conclusion: "Often DP, or Backtracking + pruning, depending on constraints." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "28. Backtracking vs DP — the bridge", dark: true,
        body: "One of the most important concepts for your overall interview preparation.\n\nStart with: choices -> DFS -> Backtracking. Then notice: same state appears repeatedly. Add: memoization.\n\nNow: Backtracking + Memoization = Top-down DP.\n\nOr formulate: state transition, and build: Bottom-up DP.",
        conclusion: "This is how I want you to think about DP rather than memorizing 50 DP formulas." },
      { type: "variants", heading: "29-32. Common mistakes", items: [
        { id: "1", title: "Mistake #1 — backtrack(i+1) for Combination Sum I", note: "Wrong because reuse is allowed. Correct: backtrack(i)." },
        { id: "2", title: "Mistake #2 — duplicate check without i > start", note: "if (candidates[i]==candidates[i-1]) without i > start can incorrectly remove valid duplicate selections such as [1,1]. Correct: if (i > start && candidates[i]==candidates[i-1])." },
        { id: "3", title: "Mistake #3 — remaining < 0 pruning with negative candidates", note: "That pruning is invalid when candidates can be negative. Always verify the mathematical assumption behind pruning." },
        { id: "4", title: "Mistake #4 — using backtracking for Combination Sum IV", note: "If order matters and you only need a count, DP is usually the right direction." }
      ]},
      { type: "diagram", heading: "33. Recognition cheat sheet", dark: true,
        body: "Combination Sum I -> reuse, order doesn't matter -> backtrack(i)\n\nCombination Sum II -> no reuse, duplicates, sort + skip -> backtrack(i+1)\n\nCombination Sum III -> exactly K, numbers 1..9 -> backtrack(i+1)\n\nCombination Sum IV -> order matters, count -> DP" },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "34. Master template", dark: true,
        body: "For most combination-generation problems:\n\nvoid backtrack(int start, int remaining) {\n\n    if (isSolution()) {\n        result.add(new ArrayList<>(path));\n        return;\n    }\n\n    for (int i = start; i < n; i++) {\n\n        if (shouldPrune(i, remaining)) {\n            break;\n        }\n\n        if (shouldSkipDuplicate(i, start)) {\n            continue;\n        }\n\n        path.add(nums[i]);\n\n        backtrack(\n            nextStart(i),\n            remaining - nums[i]\n        );\n\n        path.remove(path.size() - 1);\n    }\n}\n\nThe entire problem is often just deciding: nextStart(i) -- is it i or i+1?" },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "35. The ultimate decision tree", dark: true,
        body: "When an interviewer says: \"choose numbers to reach a target.\" Think:\n\n                    TARGET\n                      |\n                What is required?\n                      |\n             +--------+--------+\n             v                 v\n        Return all          Count ways\n             |                 |\n             v                 v\n       Backtracking           DP?\n             |\n        Does order matter?\n         /            \\\n       NO              YES\n       |                |\n  Combination       Often DP\n       |\n   Reuse allowed?\n    /        \\\n  YES        NO\n   |          |\n backtrack(i) backtrack(i+1)" },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "36. Muscle-memory card", dark: true,
        body: "When you see: \"Find all combinations that sum to target.\" write: path, start, remaining.\n\nThen: for i = start -> n: choose, recurse, undo.\n\nAsk: Reuse? YES -> recurse(i). NO -> recurse(i+1).\nAsk: Duplicates? YES -> sort + skip same-level duplicates.\nAsk: Positive numbers? YES -> remaining < 0 can prune.\nAsk: Order matters + count? -> Think DP." },
      { type: "checklist", heading: "Tier 5 progress",
        items: [{ label: "37. Basic Trie", done: true }, { label: "38. Trie + Prefix Search", done: true },
          { label: "39. Trie + DFS", done: true }, { label: "40. Trie + Backtracking", done: true },
          { label: "41. Trie + Wildcard", done: true }, { label: "42. Trie + Frequency", done: true },
          { label: "43. Bitwise Trie", done: false }, { label: "44. Basic Backtracking", done: true },
          { label: "45. Subsets", done: true }, { label: "46. Permutations", done: true },
          { label: "47. Combinations", done: true }, { label: "48. Combination Sum", done: true },
          { label: "49. Partitioning", done: false }, { label: "50. Grid Backtracking", done: false },
          { label: "51. Constraint Backtracking", done: false }, { label: "52. Backtracking + Trie", done: false },
          { label: "53. Backtracking + Bitmask", done: false }] },
      { type: "nextLesson", pattern: "Partitioning",
        points: ["Another very common form of backtracking: String -> Choose a cut -> Validate substring -> Recurse on remaining suffix -> Undo", "We'll use Palindrome Partitioning to learn the general pattern, then connect it to Word Break, string DP, and how to recognize when partitioning should become DP instead of backtracking"] }
    ]
  },

  "partitioning": {
    title: "Pattern 49 — Partitioning: Strings, Cut Points & the Backtracking->DP Bridge (Must Master)",
    sections: [
      { type: "callout", heading: "The core idea", tone: "gold",
        body: "A very important pattern because it connects Strings -> Backtracking -> DP.",
        conclusion: "The core idea is surprisingly simple: at every position, choose where to cut the string." },
      { type: "text", heading: "1. Why does partitioning exist?",
        body: "Suppose s = \"aab\". We want to partition it into pieces where every piece is a palindrome. Possible partitions: [\"a\",\"a\",\"b\"], [\"aa\",\"b\"]. We need to decide: where should I cut? At index 0, possible first pieces are \"a\", \"aa\", \"aab\". We test each -- only \"a\" and \"aa\" are valid palindromes. Then recursively solve the remaining suffix." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "2. Mental model", dark: true,
        body: "STRING -> Choose a prefix -> Is prefix valid? -> YES -> Solve remaining suffix.\n\nFor \"aab\":\n                         \"\"\n                    /     |      \\\n                  \"a\"    \"aa\"    \"aab\"\n                   v       v       x\n                  /         \\\n                \"a\"         \"b\"\n                 v           v\n\nSolutions: [\"a\",\"a\",\"b\"], [\"aa\",\"b\"]" },
      { type: "chips", heading: "3. Recognition triggers",
        intro: "Think Partitioning Backtracking when you see:",
        items: ["partition a string", "split string into pieces", "divide string into valid segments", "all possible partitions", "palindrome partitioning", "split into substrings satisfying a condition", "restore possible expressions/IP addresses", "break string into valid words"],
        note: "The common structure: choose a substring -> validate -> recurse on remaining suffix." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "4-5. The universal partition template & why end+1", dark: true,
        body: "void backtrack(int start) {\n\n    if (start == s.length()) {\n        result.add(new ArrayList<>(path));\n        return;\n    }\n\n    for (int end = start; end < s.length(); end++) {\n\n        String part = s.substring(start, end + 1);\n\n        if (!isValid(part)) {\n            continue;\n        }\n\n        path.add(part);\n\n        backtrack(end + 1);\n\n        path.remove(path.size() - 1);\n    }\n}\n\nWe try every possible endpoint. Why end+1? If you choose \"a\" (index 0), the remaining string begins at index 1. If you choose \"aa\" (indices 0,1), remaining starts at 2.",
        conclusion: "end + 1 moves the partition boundary past the chosen substring." },
      { type: "text", heading: "6. The state",
        body: "Partitioning usually needs: start, path. Sometimes additional state: remaining, numberOfParts, currentValue, depending on the problem. But the fundamental state is: start = where the unprocessed suffix begins." },
      { type: "text", heading: "7. Palindrome Partitioning walkthrough",
        body: "Example s=\"aab\". Try \"a\": palindrome? YES. Then backtrack(1). Remaining \"ab\". Try \"a\": valid. Then \"b\": valid. Result: [\"a\",\"a\",\"b\"]. Backtrack. At root also try \"aa\": valid. Remaining \"b\": valid. Result: [\"aa\",\"b\"]." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "8-9. Validation function & avoiding substring creation", dark: true,
       body: "boolean isPalindrome(String s, int left, int right) {\n\n    while (left < right) {\n\n        if (s.charAt(left) != s.charAt(right)) {\n            return false;\n        }\n\n        left++;\n        right--;\n    }\n\n    return true;\n}\n\nThen: if (!isPalindrome(s, start, end)) continue;\n\nBetter: validate directly with isPalindrome(s, start, end) before creating the substring object, then only call path.add(s.substring(start, end+1)) once the range is confirmed valid. This avoids unnecessary substring creation during validation." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "10. Complete Java solution", dark: true,
        body: "class Solution {\n\n    List<List<String>> result = new ArrayList<>();\n    List<String> path = new ArrayList<>();\n\n    public List<List<String>> partition(String s) {\n\n        backtrack(s, 0);\n\n        return result;\n    }\n\n    private void backtrack(String s, int start) {\n\n        if (start == s.length()) {\n            result.add(new ArrayList<>(path));\n            return;\n        }\n\n        for (int end = start; end < s.length(); end++) {\n\n            if (!isPalindrome(s, start, end)) {\n                continue;\n            }\n\n            path.add(s.substring(start, end + 1));\n\n            backtrack(s, end + 1);\n\n            path.remove(path.size() - 1);\n        }\n    }\n}" },
      { type: "comparison", heading: "11-12. The key difference from combinations", items: [
        { label: "Combination", body: "choose nums[i] -- the choice is one ELEMENT." },
        { label: "Partitioning", body: "choose substring [start...end] -- the choice is one RANGE." }
      ], conclusion: "This is the central partitioning idea. Think: start -> end=start -> end=start+1 -> end=start+2 -> ... At each end, [start...end] is a candidate piece. Partitioning = Backtracking over possible cut positions." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "13. Partitioning vs Substring", tone: "gold",
        body: "Suppose \"abc\". Substrings: \"a\",\"ab\",\"abc\",\"b\",\"bc\",\"c\". Partitioning asks: which collection of substrings covers the ENTIRE string, in order? [\"a\",\"bc\"] is a partition. But [\"a\",\"c\"] is not a complete partition because \"b\" is missing.",
        conclusion: "This is why recursion always continues from end + 1." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "14-15. Partition into exactly K parts / with maximum length", tone: "gold",
        body: "Suppose: split string into exactly 3 pieces. State: start, partsUsed. Termination: start==n && partsUsed==k. Prune if: remainingCharacters < remainingParts, because every part needs at least one character. This is: Partitioning + Constraint + Pruning.\n\nSuppose: split the string into pieces of length at most 3. Then: for (int end = start; end < n && end < start + 3; end++). This limits the branching factor.",
        conclusion: "Constraints reduce the search space." },
      { type: "text", heading: "16-17. Pattern: Restore IP Addresses",
        body: "Classic partitioning problem. Given \"25525511135\", return valid IP addresses: 255.255.11.135, 255.255.111.35. The IP has exactly 4 parts, each 0..255, leading zeros restricted. This is exactly: choose substring -> validate -> recurse.\n\nMental model: at each step, choose 1 digit, 2 digits, or 3 digits. Validate: 0 <= value <= 255, and \"01\" is invalid. Then backtrack(end+1) until 4 parts are formed." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "18. Restore IP template", dark: true,
        body: "void backtrack(int start) {\n\n    if (path.size() == 4) {\n\n        if (start == s.length()) {\n            result.add(String.join(\".\", path));\n        }\n\n        return;\n    }\n\n    for (int end = start; end < s.length(); end++) {\n\n        if (end - start + 1 > 3) {\n            break;\n        }\n\n        String part = s.substring(start, end + 1);\n\n        if (!isValid(part)) {\n            continue;\n        }\n\n        path.add(part);\n\n        backtrack(end + 1);\n\n        path.remove(path.size() - 1);\n    }\n}\n\nSame partitioning engine. Different validation." },
      { type: "variants", heading: "19. This is the deep pattern", items: [
        { id: "1", title: "Palindrome Partitioning", note: "choice = substring, validation = palindrome" },
        { id: "2", title: "IP Restoration", note: "choice = substring, validation = valid IP segment" },
        { id: "3", title: "Expression generation", note: "choice = substring, validation = valid number/operator" },
        { id: "4", title: "Word partitioning", note: "choice = substring, validation = dictionary contains word" }
      ], conclusion: "Partitioning = choose a substring + validate it + recurse." },
      { type: "text", heading: "20. Pattern: Word Break",
        body: "Suppose s=\"leetcode\", dictionary=[\"leet\",\"code\"]. Can we split \"leet\" + \"code\"? Backtracking solution: start=0, choose \"l\",\"le\",\"lee\",\"leet\" (valid!) -> backtrack(4) -> choose \"c\",\"co\",\"cod\",\"code\" (valid!). Result: true. But there's a major problem." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "21. Why Word Break often becomes DP", tone: "gold",
        body: "Imagine many paths reach start=7. The remaining suffix is exactly the same. path A -> start 7, path B -> start 7, path C -> start 7. From that point onward, the future possibilities are identical. So why solve it three times? Memoize: memo[start].",
        conclusion: "This turns Backtracking + Memoization = DP. One of the most important bridges between the patterns you're learning." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "22. Partitioning -> DP recognition", tone: "gold",
        body: "When partitioning a string, ask: does the result depend only on the current start position? If yes, there may be a DP solution.",
        conclusion: "Example: Word Break -- state dp[start] or dp[i], meaning: can suffix/prefix from this position be successfully segmented?" },
      { type: "callout", heading: "23. Palindrome Partitioning II", tone: "red",
        body: "Different problem: what is the minimum number of cuts needed to partition a string into palindromes? Example \"aab\" -> answer 1 (\"aa\" | \"b\"). Now we're not returning every partition -- we're optimizing minimum cuts.",
        conclusion: "This strongly suggests: DP. You can still think about it as partitioning first, then derive DP." },
      { type: "variants", heading: "24. Backtracking vs DP for partitioning", items: [
        { id: "1", title: "Return all partitions", note: "Backtracking" },
        { id: "2", title: "Determine whether a partition exists", note: "Backtracking or DP" },
        { id: "3", title: "Count partitions", note: "DP candidate" },
        { id: "4", title: "Minimum/maximum cuts", note: "DP" },
        { id: "5", title: "Same suffix state repeats", note: "Memoization / DP" }
      ], conclusion: "This distinction is very important." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "25-26. Optimization: precompute palindromes", dark: true,
        body: "For Palindrome Partitioning, checking isPalindrome(start, end) repeatedly costs time. Precompute: palindrome[start][end]. Then validation becomes O(1) after preprocessing.\n\nA substring s[i...j] is a palindrome if s[i]==s[j] and (j-i<=1 or palindrome[i+1][j-1]==true).\n\npal[i][j] =\n    s.charAt(i) == s.charAt(j)\n    &&\n    (j - i <= 1 || pal[i + 1][j - 1]);\n\nNow Backtracking + Palindrome DP becomes a powerful combination." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "27. Why this matters for you", tone: "gold",
        body: "You're currently learning patterns separately: Arrays, Strings, Backtracking, DP. But strong interview problem solving requires seeing: Pattern A -> what state repeats? -> Memoize -> Pattern B.\n\nFor partitioning: Backtracking -> same start index repeats -> memoization -> DP.",
        conclusion: "This is exactly how you should learn DP." },
      { type: "text", heading: "28. Pattern: Partition + Trie",
        body: "Suppose: split a string into dictionary words. Instead of checking every substring against a HashSet, you can use a Trie. Process: start -> Trie traversal -> word found? -> backtrack(nextIndex). So: String Partitioning + Trie + Backtracking. A very common advanced combination." },
      { type: "text", heading: "29. Pattern: Partition + HashSet",
        body: "Simpler Word Break: dictionary = HashSet<String>. At every endpoint: if (dict.contains(substring)) then recurse. This is: Backtracking + HashSet. Memoization may then turn it into DP." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "30-31. Pattern: Expression Add Operators", tone: "gold",
        body: "Given \"123\", target=6. Possible: 1+2+3=6, 1*2*3=6. At each position you choose a number substring then optionally + - *. So the pattern becomes: Partition substring + operator choice + expression state + backtracking.\n\nWhy lastOperand? To correctly handle multiplication precedence. For example, 1+2*3: when multiplying, you need to replace +2 with +2*3. So the state becomes more sophisticated.",
        conclusion: "The backtracking engine stays the same; only the state changes." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "32. Partitioning recognition map", dark: true,
        body: "                 STRING\n                   |\n             Split into pieces?\n                   |\n                  YES\n                   |\n          Choose substring\n                   |\n              Validate\n                   |\n              +----+----+\n              v         v\n            Valid     Invalid\n              |          |\n              v          v\n           Recurse      Skip\n              |\n             Undo\n\nThat is the entire pattern." },
      { type: "comparison", heading: "33. Partitioning vs Sliding Window", items: [
        { label: "Find the longest substring satisfying condition", body: "Think: Sliding Window." },
        { label: "Split the entire string into valid pieces in every possible way", body: "Think: Partitioning Backtracking." },
        { label: "Can the string be split into dictionary words?", body: "Think: Word Break DP / Backtracking + memo." }
      ], conclusion: "Different wording -> different search model." },
      { type: "comparison", heading: "34. Partitioning vs Subsequence", items: [
        { label: "Subsequence", body: "You decide: take / skip character. For \"abc\": take a/skip a, take b/skip b, ..." },
        { label: "Partitioning", body: "You decide: where to cut. For \"abc\": \"a\"|\"bc\", \"ab\"|\"c\", \"a\"|\"b\"|\"c\", \"abc\"." }
      ], conclusion: "Subsequence -> character decision. Partition -> boundary decision. A powerful distinction." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "35. Muscle-memory card", dark: true,
        body: "When you see: \"Partition / split the string into valid pieces.\" Immediately think: start -> for end = start -> n-1 -> substring(start,end) -> valid? -> choose -> backtrack(end+1) -> undo.\n\nThen ask:\nAll partitions? -> Backtracking\nCount partitions? -> DP candidate\nMinimum/maximum cuts? -> DP\nSame start state repeats? -> Memoization\nDictionary words? -> HashSet / Trie + DP/backtracking\nPalindrome? -> palindrome check / palindrome DP" },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "36. Master partition template", dark: true,
        body: "void backtrack(int start) {\n\n    if (start == n) {\n        result.add(new ArrayList<>(path));\n        return;\n    }\n\n    for (int end = start; end < n; end++) {\n\n        if (!valid(start, end)) {\n            continue;\n        }\n\n        path.add(s.substring(start, end + 1));\n\n        backtrack(end + 1);\n\n        path.remove(path.size() - 1);\n    }\n}\n\nThe only thing that changes from problem to problem is valid(start, end) and sometimes additional state." },
      { type: "practice", heading: "37. Interview answer",
        intro: "If asked: \"how would you solve palindrome partitioning?\"",
        items: [
          "\"I'll use backtracking where the state is the current start index and the current partition. At each step I'll try every possible substring starting at that index. If the substring is a palindrome, I'll add it to the current path and recurse from the next index. After recursion I'll undo the choice. If needed, I can precompute palindrome ranges using DP to make palindrome checks O(1).\""
        ],
        note: "That's exactly the kind of explanation you want to practice." },
      { type: "checklist", heading: "Tier 5 progress",
        items: [{ label: "37. Basic Trie", done: true }, { label: "38. Trie + Prefix Search", done: true },
          { label: "39. Trie + DFS", done: true }, { label: "40. Trie + Backtracking", done: true },
          { label: "41. Trie + Wildcard", done: true }, { label: "42. Trie + Frequency", done: true },
          { label: "43. Bitwise Trie", done: false }, { label: "44. Basic Backtracking", done: true },
          { label: "45. Subsets", done: true }, { label: "46. Permutations", done: true },
          { label: "47. Combinations", done: true }, { label: "48. Combination Sum", done: true },
          { label: "49. Partitioning", done: true }, { label: "50. Grid Backtracking", done: false },
          { label: "51. Constraint Backtracking", done: false }, { label: "52. Backtracking + Trie", done: false },
          { label: "53. Backtracking + Bitmask", done: false }] },
      { type: "nextLesson", pattern: "Grid Backtracking",
        points: ["Problems like Word Search, Word Search II, Maze, Rat in a Maze, Unique Paths with obstacles/constraints, Hamiltonian-style paths", "The mental model changes slightly: Current cell -> 4 possible neighbors -> Choose -> Mark visited -> DFS -> Unmark visited", "The key new muscle will be state restoration on a grid, and how Trie + Grid Backtracking combines to solve Word Search II efficiently"] }
    ]
  },

  "grid-backtracking": {
    title: "Pattern 50 — Grid Backtracking: DFS + Visited + Undo on a Grid (Must Master)",
    sections: [
      { type: "callout", heading: "The core idea", tone: "gold",
        body: "One of the highest-value backtracking patterns for interviews. The important insight: a grid is just a graph where each cell is a node and neighboring cells are edges.",
        conclusion: "So Grid Backtracking is essentially: DFS + choices + visited state + undo." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "1. Why does Grid Backtracking exist?", dark: true,
        body: "Suppose:\nA B C\nD E F\nG H I\n\nStarting from E, possible moves might be:\n    B\n    |\nD - E - F\n    |\n    H\n\nAt every cell, you have multiple choices. For normal DFS: visit neighbor. For backtracking: visit neighbor -> mark visited -> explore -> unmark visited.",
        conclusion: "That final unmark step is crucial." },
      { type: "chips", heading: "2. Recognition triggers",
        intro: "Think Grid Backtracking when you see:",
        items: ["Word Search", "find a path in a matrix", "visit cells under constraints", "can move up/down/left/right", "find a word in a board", "generate paths through a grid", "visit every required cell", "maze with constraints", "cannot reuse a cell", "find all valid paths"],
        note: "Especially: \"you can move from one cell to neighboring cells and cannot reuse a cell.\" Think: DFS + Backtracking." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "3-4. The four directions & basic grid DFS", dark: true,
        body: "int[][] dirs = {\n    {1, 0},\n    {-1, 0},\n    {0, 1},\n    {0, -1}\n};\n// down, up, right, left\n\nfor (int[] dir : dirs) {\n\n    int nr = r + dir[0];\n    int nc = c + dir[1];\n\n}\n\nBasic template:\nvoid dfs(int r, int c) {\n\n    if (invalid(r, c)) {\n        return;\n    }\n\n    visited[r][c] = true;\n\n    for (int[] dir : dirs) {\n\n        int nr = r + dir[0];\n        int nc = c + dir[1];\n\n        dfs(nr, nc);\n    }\n\n    visited[r][c] = false;\n}\n\nThe final visited[r][c] = false; is the backtracking step." },
      { type: "text", heading: "5. The three checks",
        body: "For every neighbor: 1. Boundary (nr<0, nr>=rows, nc<0, nc>=cols). 2. Already visited (visited[nr][nc]). 3. Problem-specific condition, e.g. board[nr][nc]==requiredCharacter. Only if all are valid do we recurse." },
      { type: "text", heading: "6. Word Search — classic example",
        body: "Suppose board = A B C E / S F C S / A D E E, word = \"ABCCED\". We need to find whether the word exists. Rules: move up/down/left/right, each cell can be used once. This is exactly Grid Backtracking." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "7. Mental model for Word Search", tone: "gold",
        body: "Suppose we match A. Now need B -- choose neighboring B. Then need C -- choose neighboring C. If we reach a dead end (wrong path), we backtrack.",
        conclusion: "At each point: which neighboring cell can satisfy the next character?" },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "8. Word Search template", dark: true,
        body: "boolean dfs(int r, int c, int index) {\n\n    if (index == word.length()) {\n        return true;\n    }\n\n    if (r < 0 || r >= rows ||\n        c < 0 || c >= cols) {\n        return false;\n    }\n\n    if (visited[r][c]) {\n        return false;\n    }\n\n    if (board[r][c] != word.charAt(index)) {\n        return false;\n    }\n\n    visited[r][c] = true;\n\n    for (int[] dir : dirs) {\n\n        int nr = r + dir[0];\n        int nc = c + dir[1];\n\n        if (dfs(nr, nc, index + 1)) {\n            return true;\n        }\n    }\n\n    visited[r][c] = false;\n\n    return false;\n}\n\nAnd try every cell as a starting point." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "9. Why index is state", tone: "gold",
        body: "We need to know: which character of the word am I currently matching? Example: index=0 -> A, index=1 -> B, index=2 -> C.",
        conclusion: "Therefore Grid Backtracking state is often: (row, col, index, visited)." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "10-11. The most important undo & why it differs from normal graph DFS", tone: "red",
        body: "visited[r][c] = true; then after exploring: visited[r][c] = false;. Why? Because the same cell may be used in a DIFFERENT path. Example: Path A: A->B->C. Path B: A->D->C. The C cell can be reused across different paths -- it just cannot be reused within the same path.\n\nNormal graph traversal: visited[node]=true, usually never unmark, because you're trying to visit every reachable node once. Backtracking: mark -> explore this path -> unmark, because you're exploring different candidate paths.",
        conclusion: "This distinction is extremely important." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "12. Alternative: modify the board", dark: true,
        body: "You don't necessarily need boolean[][] visited. You can temporarily modify the cell:\n\nchar temp = board[r][c];\nboard[r][c] = '#';\n\ndfs(...);\n\nboard[r][c] = temp;\n\nThis saves memory. The principle remains: mutate -> explore -> restore. Again: Choose -> Explore -> Undo." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "13. Word Search complexity", tone: "gold",
        body: "Suppose M x N board and word length L. You can start from every cell (M x N). At each step there can be up to 4 directions.",
        conclusion: "So worst-case roughly: O(M x N x 4^L), with visited restrictions reducing the actual branching factor." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "14. Pattern: Count All Paths", dark: true,
        body: "Suppose: count the number of paths from top-left to bottom-right. Instead of return true, you accumulate: count += dfs(...).\n\nint dfs(int r, int c) {\n\n    if (r == rows - 1 &&\n        c == cols - 1) {\n        return 1;\n    }\n\n    int count = 0;\n\n    for (...) {\n        count += dfs(...);\n    }\n\n    return count;\n}\n\nBut whether this is backtracking or DP depends on whether states repeat." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "15-16. Grid DP vs Grid Backtracking", tone: "red",
        body: "Suppose movement is only right/down. Then from a cell (r,c), the future depends only on (r,c). Many paths can reach the same cell. Therefore DP[r][c] is usually appropriate.\n\nBut if you cannot revisit cells and the set of visited cells matters, then (r,c,visited) may be required -- that can become exponential.\n\nHuge recognition rule: if future depends only on position -> Grid DP. If future depends on the path already taken -> Backtracking.",
        conclusion: "This is one of the most important DP-vs-backtracking distinctions." },
      { type: "text", heading: "17-18. Word Search II & why Trie",
        body: "Now the problem becomes: given a board and 10,000 words, find all words present in the board. Doing \"for every word: run Word Search\" would be expensive. Instead: Trie + Grid Backtracking.\n\nSuppose words [\"oath\",\"oat\",\"oats\",\"eat\"]. During board traversal o->a->t, Trie tells us \"oat\" is a prefix, so continue. But if we get o->a->z and Trie has no \"oaz...\", we immediately stop. This is powerful pruning." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "19. Word Search II mental model", dark: true,
        body: "Board -> Start cell -> Trie traversal -> Does prefix exist?\n  NO -> PRUNE\n  YES -> Is complete word? -> record -> explore neighbors\n\nThis is: Trie + Backtracking + DFS + Pruning." },
      { type: "text", heading: "20. Why this is powerful",
        body: "Without Trie: try every word. With Trie: search shared prefixes once. For example apple, app, application, apply share \"app\" -- Trie lets all of them share that traversal." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "21. Grid + Trie state", tone: "gold",
        body: "The state becomes: row, column, TrieNode, visited. Notice: we don't need index, because the Trie node tells us where we are in the word prefix.",
        conclusion: "That's an important pattern recognition point." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "22-23. Word Search II template & why node.word=null", dark: true,
        body: "void dfs(\n    int r,\n    int c,\n    TrieNode node\n) {\n\n    if (invalid(r, c)) {\n        return;\n    }\n\n    char ch = board[r][c];\n\n    if (node.children[ch - 'a'] == null) {\n        return;\n    }\n\n    node = node.children[ch - 'a'];\n\n    if (node.word != null) {\n        result.add(node.word);\n        node.word = null;\n    }\n\n    board[r][c] = '#';\n\n    for (int[] dir : dirs) {\n        dfs(\n            r + dir[0],\n            c + dir[1],\n            node\n        );\n    }\n\n    board[r][c] = ch;\n}\n\nThe important ideas: Trie prefix check + mark visited + DFS + restore.\n\nWhy set node.word = null? Suppose we find \"oath\" from one path. Another path might discover the same word -- we only want it once. So node.word=null acts as a deduplication mechanism. Alternatively use a Set<String>." },
      { type: "text", heading: "24. Pattern: Maze",
        body: "Suppose 0=open, 1=blocked, find a path from start to destination. At each cell: up, down, left, right. If valid: mark, DFS, unmark. Same exact Grid Backtracking pattern." },
      { type: "text", heading: "25. Pattern: Hamiltonian Path",
        body: "Now: visit every vertex exactly once. This is essentially: Graph + visited + Backtracking. State: currentNode, visited, path. At every step: choose an unvisited neighbor. If all nodes are visited: success. This connects grid backtracking to general graph problems." },
      { type: "text", heading: "26. Pattern: Unique Paths With Constraints",
        body: "Suppose: find all paths from start to end while avoiding obstacles and visiting certain cells. Then: current position + visited + constraints. Again: DFS + backtracking." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "27. Critical distinction: Backtracking vs BFS", tone: "red",
        body: "Suppose the problem asks: find the SHORTEST path in an unweighted grid. Don't immediately use backtracking. Use BFS, because BFS explores by distance: distance 0, 1, 2, 3, ...",
        conclusion: "The first time you reach the target is the shortest path." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "28. Grid pattern decision tree", dark: true,
        body: "              GRID PATH\n                  |\n             What do they ask?\n                  |\n        +---------+---------+\n        v         v         v\n     Shortest   All paths   Count paths\n        |         |           |\n       BFS     Backtracking   |\n                              |\n                     Repeated states?\n                       /          \\\n                     YES           NO\n                      |             |\n                     DP        Backtracking\n\nThis is an extremely useful interview decision tree." },
      { type: "text", heading: "29. If movement has cycles",
        body: "Suppose up/down/left/right. You can go A -> B -> A -> B... So you need visited or temporarily mark cells. This prevents infinite recursion." },
      { type: "variants", heading: "30-33. Common mistakes", items: [
        { id: "1", title: "Mistake #1 — forgetting to restore visited[r][c]=false", note: "Then another path can't use the cell. Remember: mark -> explore -> UNMARK." },
        { id: "2", title: "Mistake #2 — marking visited too late", note: "Wrong: dfs(neighbor); visited[current]=true; -- by then recursion may already have revisited the cell. Correct: visited[current]=true; dfs(neighbor); visited[current]=false;" },
        { id: "3", title: "Mistake #3 — forgetting boundaries", note: "Always check: if (r<0 || r>=rows || c<0 || c>=cols) return; This should become automatic." },
        { id: "4", title: "Mistake #4 — using a global visited without understanding path scope", note: "For backtracking, visited represents cells used by the current path -- must be restored. For normal connected-component DFS, visited means cells already processed globally -- don't restore. This distinction is crucial." }
      ]},
      { type: "callout", heading: "34. Complexity thinking", tone: "gold",
        body: "For a board M x N and maximum path length L, rough upper bound: O(M x N x 4^L) for path exploration. With Trie pruning, many branches disappear and practical performance can improve substantially.",
        conclusion: "When discussing complexity in interviews, don't claim Trie magically changes the worst-case exponential nature of unrestricted path search. It primarily reduces the search space by pruning invalid prefixes early." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "35. The deep pattern", dark: true,
        body: "Grid Backtracking isn't really a new algorithm. It's: BACKTRACKING + GRID NEIGHBORS.\n\nThe generic structure:\nChoose a neighbor -> Validate -> Mark state -> Explore -> Undo state\n\nThat's all." },
      { type: "variants", heading: "36. Compound patterns you should recognize", items: [
        { id: "1", title: "Grid + Backtracking", note: "Word Search, Maze, Path constraints" },
        { id: "2", title: "Grid + Trie + Backtracking", note: "Word Search II" },
        { id: "3", title: "Grid + BFS", note: "Shortest path, minimum steps, minimum moves" },
        { id: "4", title: "Grid + DP", note: "Count paths, maximum path value, minimum path cost" },
        { id: "5", title: "Grid + DFS", note: "Connected components, islands, reachability" }
      ], conclusion: "This is the classification you want to build." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "37. Muscle-memory card", dark: true,
        body: "When you see: \"Find a word/path in a grid without reusing cells.\" Immediately think: DFS + visited + backtracking.\n\nTemplate:\nif invalid:\n    return\n\nmark current\n\nfor each neighbor:\n    dfs(neighbor)\n\nunmark current\n\nIf there are many words: Trie + Grid Backtracking.\nIf they ask for shortest path: BFS.\nIf they ask for number of paths and state repeats: DP." },
      { type: "checklist", heading: "Tier 5 progress",
        items: [{ label: "37. Basic Trie", done: true }, { label: "38. Trie + Prefix Search", done: true },
          { label: "39. Trie + DFS", done: true }, { label: "40. Trie + Backtracking", done: true },
          { label: "41. Trie + Wildcard", done: true }, { label: "42. Trie + Frequency", done: true },
          { label: "43. Bitwise Trie", done: false }, { label: "44. Basic Backtracking", done: true },
          { label: "45. Subsets", done: true }, { label: "46. Permutations", done: true },
          { label: "47. Combinations", done: true }, { label: "48. Combination Sum", done: true },
          { label: "49. Partitioning", done: true }, { label: "50. Grid Backtracking", done: true },
          { label: "51. Constraint Backtracking", done: false }, { label: "52. Backtracking + Trie", done: false },
          { label: "53. Backtracking + Bitmask", done: false }] },
      { type: "nextLesson", pattern: "Constraint Backtracking",
        points: ["The general-purpose form of backtracking, using problems such as N-Queens, Sudoku, Graph Coloring, Constraint placement", "The most important advanced concept: don't blindly explore the tree -- at every choice, immediately test whether the partial solution can still become valid", "Choose -> Validate -> Prune -> Explore -> Undo -- one of the most important interview muscles for hard problems"] }
    ]
  },

  "constraint-backtracking": {
    title: "Pattern 51 — Constraint Backtracking: Choose, Validate, Prune, Explore, Undo (Must Master)",
    sections: [
      { type: "callout", heading: "The core idea", tone: "gold",
        body: "This is the point where backtracking moves from \"generate every possibility\" to \"generate possibilities, but kill impossible branches as early as possible.\" One of the most important patterns for hard coding interviews.",
        conclusion: "Choose -> Validate -> Prune -> Explore -> Undo." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "1. Why does Constraint Backtracking exist?", dark: true,
        body: "Consider: place 8 queens on an 8x8 chessboard so no two queens attack each other. A naive approach -- try every possible placement -- would create an enormous search space.\n\nInstead:\nChoose a position\n        v\nCheck constraints immediately\n        v\nInvalid?\n   YES -> PRUNE\n   NO  -> continue\n        v\nPlace next queen" },
      { type: "comparison", heading: "2. Mental model", items: [
        { label: "Normal backtracking", body: "Choose -> Explore -> Undo." },
        { label: "Constraint backtracking", body: "Choose -> Is this choice valid? -> NO: stop this branch. YES: Explore -> Undo." }
      ], conclusion: "The difference is the early validation." },
      { type: "chips", heading: "3. Recognition triggers",
        intro: "Think Constraint Backtracking when you see:",
        items: ["place objects under rules", "arrange things so conditions are satisfied", "no two elements can conflict", "fill a board", "Sudoku", "N-Queens", "graph coloring", "scheduling", "assign resources", "generate valid configurations", "find all valid arrangements", "can you satisfy all constraints?"],
        note: "Especially: \"Try choices, but reject a choice immediately if it violates a rule.\"" },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "4. The generic template", dark: true,
        body: "void backtrack(state) {\n\n    if (complete(state)) {\n        recordSolution();\n        return;\n    }\n\n    for (Choice choice : choices) {\n\n        if (!isValid(choice, state)) {\n            continue;\n        }\n\n        apply(choice, state);\n\n        backtrack(state);\n\n        undo(choice, state);\n    }\n}\n\nThis is the universal constraint-backtracking template." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "5. The four important functions", tone: "gold",
        body: "Every constraint problem can often be broken into: choices(), isValid(), apply(), undo(). This separation is extremely useful in interviews.\n\nFor N-Queens: choices = columns in current row. isValid = no queen conflict. apply = place queen. undo = remove queen.",
        conclusion: "That four-function decomposition is a strong mental model to lead with in an interview." },
      { type: "text", heading: "6-7. N-Queens & the one-queen-per-row observation",
        body: "Problem: place N queens on an N x N board so that no two queens attack each other. A queen attacks: same row, same column, same diagonal.\n\nImportant observation: we place ONE QUEEN PER ROW. Therefore row = recursion depth. At row 0, choose a column. At row 1, choose a column. And so on. This eliminates the need to consider multiple queens in the same row." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "8. N-Queens mental model", dark: true,
        body: "For N=4:\nrow 0\n v\nchoose column\n v\nrow 1\n v\nchoose column\n v\nrow 2\n v\n...\n\nEvery placement is immediately validated." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "9-10. What makes a queen placement invalid & the diagonal trick", tone: "gold",
        body: "Suppose we want (row, col). We need to check: same column (col already occupied?), main diagonal (cells have the same row-col), anti-diagonal (cells have the same row+col).\n\nFor cells (r1,c1) and (r2,c2), they are on the same diagonal if abs(r1-r2) == abs(c1-c2). Equivalent representation: main diagonal = r - c, anti-diagonal = r + c.",
        conclusion: "A very useful interview technique: convert a spatial constraint into a hashable key." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "11. Efficient N-Queens state", dark: true,
        body: "Instead of scanning the whole board every time, maintain:\n\nboolean[] cols;\nboolean[] diag1;\nboolean[] diag2;\n\nThen: cols[c], diag1[row - col], diag2[row + col] can tell us instantly whether the placement is valid." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "12. Java implementation", dark: true,
        body: "class Solution {\n\n    List<List<String>> result = new ArrayList<>();\n\n    char[][] board;\n\n    boolean[] cols;\n    boolean[] diag1;\n    boolean[] diag2;\n\n    public List<List<String>> solveNQueens(int n) {\n\n        board = new char[n][n];\n\n        for (int r = 0; r < n; r++) {\n            Arrays.fill(board[r], '.');\n        }\n\n        cols = new boolean[n];\n        diag1 = new boolean[2 * n - 1];\n        diag2 = new boolean[2 * n - 1];\n\n        backtrack(0, n);\n\n        return result;\n    }\n\n    private void backtrack(int row, int n) {\n\n        if (row == n) {\n            result.add(buildBoard());\n            return;\n        }\n\n        for (int col = 0; col < n; col++) {\n\n            int d1 = row - col + n - 1;\n            int d2 = row + col;\n\n            if (cols[col] ||\n                diag1[d1] ||\n                diag2[d2]) {\n                continue;\n            }\n\n            board[row][col] = 'Q';\n\n            cols[col] = true;\n            diag1[d1] = true;\n            diag2[d2] = true;\n\n            backtrack(row + 1, n);\n\n            board[row][col] = '.';\n\n            cols[col] = false;\n            diag1[d1] = false;\n            diag2[d2] = false;\n        }\n    }\n\n    private List<String> buildBoard() {\n\n        List<String> boardResult = new ArrayList<>();\n\n        for (char[] row : board) {\n            boardResult.add(new String(row));\n        }\n\n        return boardResult;\n    }\n}" },
      { type: "callout", heading: "13-14. Why row-col+n-1 & O(1) constraint checking", tone: "gold",
        body: "Because row - col can be negative -- for example 0 - 3 = -3, and array indexes cannot be negative. So shift it: row - col + n - 1. Now all values are 0...2n-2, hence boolean[2 * n - 1].\n\nWithout extra state, scanning previous queens could take O(N). With cols/diag1/diag2, we check in O(1).",
        conclusion: "Precompute/maintain constraint state to make validation cheap -- a very important optimization technique." },
      { type: "text", heading: "15-16. Sudoku & mental model",
        body: "Another classic constraint-backtracking problem. At each empty cell: try 1, try 2, ... try 9. For each number: is it valid in row? column? 3x3 box? If valid: place -> recurse -> undo.\n\nMental model: Find empty cell -> Try candidate 1..9 -> Validate -> Invalid: skip. Valid: Place -> Solve next cell -> Undo if failure. This is exactly the same engine as N-Queens -- only the constraints change." },
      { type: "text", heading: "17. Sudoku constraints",
        body: "For a cell (r,c) and number x, check: row[r], column[c], box[r/3][c/3]. If any contains x: invalid. Otherwise: place." },
      { type: "variants", heading: "18. The bigger pattern -- same engine, different isValid()", items: [
        { id: "1", title: "N-Queens", note: "choice = column, constraint = columns + diagonals" },
        { id: "2", title: "Sudoku", note: "choice = number, constraint = row + column + box" },
        { id: "3", title: "Graph Coloring", note: "choice = color, constraint = neighboring colors" },
        { id: "4", title: "Scheduling", note: "choice = time/resource, constraint = conflicts/capacity" }
      ], conclusion: "Same engine. Different isValid()." },
      { type: "text", heading: "19. Graph Coloring",
        body: "Suppose a Graph and K colors. Assign each node a color such that adjacent nodes have different colors. At node v: try color 0, try color 1, ... try color K-1. For each: check neighbors. If no neighbor has that color: assign, recurse. Otherwise: skip. Again: Choose -> Validate -> Explore -> Undo." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "20-21. Constraint propagation", tone: "red",
        body: "A more advanced idea. Instead of only checking whether the current choice is valid, ask: does this choice make a future solution impossible?\n\nExample Sudoku: if placing a number causes another empty cell to have zero possible values, immediately backtrack. This is stronger than simple validation -- it is called constraint propagation.\n\nExample: suppose Sudoku has an empty cell that can only accept {5}. Place 5. Now another cell might become {} -- no valid number. Therefore the current branch is impossible. Backtrack immediately.",
        conclusion: "This can dramatically reduce search." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "22-23. Most Constrained Variable (MRV)", tone: "gold",
        body: "A very powerful advanced technique: choose the variable with the fewest available options. Instead of filling the first empty Sudoku cell, choose the empty cell with minimum candidates.\n\nSuppose Cell A -> 6 possible numbers, Cell B -> 2 possible numbers, Cell C -> 1 possible number. Choose Cell C first. This is called MRV -- Minimum Remaining Values.\n\nWhy MRV works: if a variable has only one possible value, that's a forced decision. If that decision leads to contradiction, fail immediately instead of exploring thousands of irrelevant branches first.",
        conclusion: "Same philosophy as pruning: fail as early as possible." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "24. Forward checking", tone: "gold",
        body: "Another advanced technique: after assigning variable = value, remove that value from neighboring variables' domains. If any neighbor has zero possible values, backtrack immediately. This is called forward checking.",
        conclusion: "You don't necessarily need to implement sophisticated CSP algorithms in most coding interviews, but understanding the concept helps with hard backtracking problems." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "25. Constraint backtracking formula", dark: true,
        body: "                 BACKTRACKING\n                      |\n                 Choose candidate\n                      |\n                 Is it valid?\n                   /     \\\n                 NO       YES\n                 |         |\n               PRUNE       v\n                       Apply choice\n                           v\n                         DFS\n                           v\n                        Undo" },
      { type: "variants", heading: "26. Constraint types you should recognize", items: [
        { id: "1", title: "Local constraint", note: "Depends only on the current choice. Example: number must be <= 9." },
        { id: "2", title: "Neighbor constraint", note: "Depends on adjacent elements. Example: adjacent colors must differ." },
        { id: "3", title: "Global constraint", note: "Depends on the whole partial solution. Example: sum <= target." },
        { id: "4", title: "Resource constraint", note: "Limited capacity. Example: at most 3 jobs per machine." },
        { id: "5", title: "Uniqueness constraint", note: "Cannot repeat. Example: no duplicate values." }
      ], conclusion: "Recognizing the constraint type helps you design the state." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "27. State design", tone: "gold",
        body: "The most important advanced skill. Ask: what information do I need to know to determine whether the next choice is valid?\n\nN-Queens: row, columns used, diagonals used. Sudoku: board, row usage, column usage, box usage. Graph coloring: current node, colors of neighbors. Scheduling: current task, resource availability, time constraints.",
        conclusion: "The state is the heart of backtracking." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "28. Backtracking vs Greedy", tone: "red",
        body: "Suppose: choose tasks to maximize profit, but tasks conflict. You might think Greedy. But if a local best choice can prevent a better global arrangement, Greedy fails. Backtracking may be necessary for small constraint spaces. However, if there is a proven greedy property, Greedy is preferable.",
        conclusion: "Never use backtracking simply because the problem \"has choices.\" Ask whether a more efficient structure exists." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "29. Backtracking vs DP", tone: "red",
        body: "Backtracking: different paths matter. DP: different paths reach same state. Example: state = (index, remaining). If many paths reach the same (index, remaining), memoization can help.",
        conclusion: "But if the state contains an entire visited set, there may still be exponentially many states." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "30. The most important optimization hierarchy", dark: true,
        body: "When solving a hard backtracking problem, think in this order:\n\nLevel 1: Can I reduce the choices?\nLevel 2: Can I validate early?\nLevel 3: Can I prune impossible branches?\nLevel 4: Can I maintain constraints in O(1)?\nLevel 5: Do states repeat? -> memoization / DP\nLevel 6: Can I choose the most constrained variable first? -> MRV\n\nThis is how you attack hard backtracking rather than simply writing brute force." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "31-32. N-Queens optimization & bitmask N-Queens", dark: true,
        body: "Naive: for every row, try every column, scan previous queens.\nBetter: maintain columns, diagonals.\nBetter search: prune immediately.\nAdvanced: bitmask columns/diagonals.\n\nNow we have: Backtracking + O(1) constraints + bitmask. This is the kind of optimization interviewers like to see when constraints are large enough.\n\nFor small N, sets such as columns, diagonal1, diagonal2 can be represented with integers. Then available positions can be computed using bit operations. This can make N-Queens extremely fast.",
        conclusion: "The important idea for now: a set of boolean constraints can sometimes be compressed into a bitmask. We'll cover this more deeply in the Backtracking + Bitmask pattern." },
      { type: "variants", heading: "33-36. Common mistakes", items: [
        { id: "1", title: "Mistake #1 — checking constraints after recursion", note: "Wrong mindset: place -> explore entire subtree -> discover invalid. Correct: candidate -> validate NOW -> reject immediately. Pruning is only useful if done early." },
        { id: "2", title: "Mistake #2 — making isValid() expensive", note: "If every candidate requires O(N) scanning, but you can maintain a HashSet/boolean[]/bitmask, you may reduce validation to O(1). Often a major performance improvement." },
        { id: "3", title: "Mistake #3 — forgetting to undo every piece of state", note: "If you set cols[col]=true, diag1[d1]=true, diag2[d2]=true, all must be restored after recursion. Backtracking requires complete state restoration." },
        { id: "4", title: "Mistake #4 — using the wrong recursion depth", note: "In N-Queens, row = recursion depth. In Sudoku, next empty cell = recursion depth. In graph coloring, node index = recursion depth. Always identify: what does one recursion level represent?" }
      ]},
      { type: "practice", heading: "37. Interview answer",
        intro: "If asked: \"how would you solve N-Queens?\"",
        items: [
          "\"I'll place one queen per row, so the recursion depth represents the current row. For each column I'll check whether that column and the two diagonals are free. I'll maintain those constraints in boolean arrays so validation is O(1). If the position is valid, I'll place the queen and recurse to the next row. After returning, I'll remove the queen and restore the constraint state.\""
        ],
        note: "That is exactly how you should explain it." },
      { type: "comparison", heading: "38. Master recognition table", items: [
        { label: "Generate all choices", body: "Backtracking" },
        { label: "Choose K", body: "Combination" },
        { label: "Order matters", body: "Permutation" },
        { label: "Split string", body: "Partitioning" },
        { label: "Move through cells", body: "Grid backtracking" },
        { label: "Placement restrictions", body: "Constraint backtracking" },
        { label: "Sudoku", body: "Constraint backtracking" },
        { label: "N-Queens", body: "Constraint backtracking" },
        { label: "Graph coloring", body: "Constraint backtracking" },
        { label: "Many choices immediately invalid", body: "Pruning" },
        { label: "Same state repeats", body: "Memoization / DP" },
        { label: "Boolean sets", body: "Bitmask candidate" }
      ] },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "39. The universal backtracking framework", dark: true,
        body: "Subsets, Permutations, Combinations, Partitioning, Grid, N-Queens, Sudoku are NOT six completely different algorithms. They are all:\n\n                 BACKTRACKING\n                      |\n                    STATE\n                      |\n                   CHOICES\n                      |\n                 VALIDATE\n                      |\n                 +----+----+\n                 |         |\n               INVALID    VALID\n                 |         |\n               PRUNE       v\n                         APPLY\n                           |\n                         DFS\n                           |\n                         UNDO\n\nOnly these change: STATE, CHOICES, VALIDATION, TERMINATION.",
        conclusion: "That is the real pattern mastery you want." },
      { type: "practice", heading: "40. Your backtracking decision tree",
        intro: "Before coding, ask these questions:",
        items: [
          "1. What represents one recursion level?",
          "2. What are my choices?",
          "3. What state must I maintain?",
          "4. What makes a choice invalid?",
          "5. Can I prune immediately?",
          "6. What state must I undo?",
          "7. Does the same state repeat?",
          "8. If yes -> Can memoization/DP help?"
        ],
        note: "If you can answer these 8 questions, you can solve a large class of backtracking problems without memorizing individual solutions." },
      { type: "checklist", heading: "Tier 5 progress",
        items: [{ label: "37. Basic Trie", done: true }, { label: "38. Trie + Prefix Search", done: true },
          { label: "39. Trie + DFS", done: true }, { label: "40. Trie + Backtracking", done: true },
          { label: "41. Trie + Wildcard", done: true }, { label: "42. Trie + Frequency", done: true },
          { label: "43. Bitwise Trie", done: false }, { label: "44. Basic Backtracking", done: true },
          { label: "45. Subsets", done: true }, { label: "46. Permutations", done: true },
          { label: "47. Combinations", done: true }, { label: "48. Combination Sum", done: true },
          { label: "49. Partitioning", done: true }, { label: "50. Grid Backtracking", done: true },
          { label: "51. Constraint Backtracking", done: true }, { label: "52. Backtracking + Trie", done: false },
          { label: "53. Backtracking + Bitmask", done: false }] },
      { type: "nextLesson", pattern: "Backtracking + Trie",
        points: ["A compound pattern, not a completely new algorithm: Trie + DFS + Backtracking + Pruning", "Uses Word Search II and related problems", "The key mental model: the grid controls where you can move; the Trie controls which prefixes are worth exploring"] }
    ]
  },
};
